  "facts": [
    {
      "id": 1,
      "text": "Cherry Valley got its name from a grove of wild cherry trees that grew along Cooper’s Creek.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        25
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.cherryvalleyar.gov/"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The town sprang to life in 1882 when the Iron Mountain Railroad’s Knobel branch crossed Cross County, turning the area into a rail hub.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        2
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/cherry_valley_arkansas"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Cherry Valley was officially incorporated on August 6, 1910, giving it formal municipal boundaries and local governance.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        11
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/cherry_valley_arkansas"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Every year in late September, the town celebrates its heritage with the Cherry Fest, featuring music, food, and community events at the local park.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        29
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/cherry_valley_arkansas"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "By the late 1880s, sawmills, a stave factory, and a cotton gin had sprung up, turning Cherry Valley into a timber and cotton processing hub.",
